SUMMARY:Merging Music & Humanity
DESCRIPTION:Turn your Sunday into a night to remember! Join us on August 9th at 7 PM for an unforgettable evening of music with one of New Orleans' most distinctive\npianists—Tom McDermott. Proceeds from this concert will directly help Global Healing's ongoing efforts in Roatán\, Honduras\, continuing to support fire recovery and providing the assistance needed to facilitate the hospital's services.\nIn 2024\, a shocking fire ravaged the sole public hospital on Roatán. Home to Global Healing's pediatric clinic for over the past two decades\, the hospital was vital for emergency care for both children and adults\, performing urgent surgeries\, and providing maternity services—like life-saving C-sections. Reduced to ashes\, the hospital and clinic left Honduras with even fewer means to deliver essential\nmedical care. All medical equipment was lost\, and recovery is still ongoing.\nEric Scher and Barbara Stowe continue to travel to Roatán often\, having helped set up a provisional hospital in a former Adventist church\, teaching interns and fellows\, and delivering our donated and purchased medical equipment/supplies—made possible by supporters and benefit concert attendees like you. So come for the music (plus Barb's famous homemade whoopie pies) and stay for the impact! Your ticket not only supports an intimate night of fantastic live music but also contributes directly to saving lives and helping the community of Roatán heal.\nAs always\, a special silent auction will be presented during the benefit-supporting Global Healing and celebrating the community members and organizations who generously donate items. If you or loved ones are interested in supporting our benefit concerts through volunteering or donating auction items for fundraising\, please contact Dr. Eric Scher at eric@globalhealing.org or (415)846-9160.\nSee you when the doors open at 6 PM! \nAbout Tom McDermott \nTom McDermott is one of New Orleans' most distinctive pianists—a modern day keeper of the city's piano lineage who moves effortlessly between ragtime\, traditional jazz\, R&B\, and Brazilian choro.\nWith a career spanning over five decades\, he embodies the Crescent City's spirit: deep-rooted in tradition yet always open to new ideas and rhythms from around the world.\nBorn in St. Louis in 1957\, McDermott began studying piano at seven and turned professional by sixteen.\nAfter earning a Master of Music degree\, he moved to New Orleans in the early 1984\, where he quickly became a go-to pianist for traditional jazz and R&B\, holding down gigs with legends and ensembles such as the Dukes of Dixieland. He has performed with New Orleans heroes such as Pete Fountain\, Irma Thomas and Gatemouth Brown. He co-founded and\narranged for the Grammy award-winning brass band\, the New Orleans Nightcrawlers. His performances with The Dukes of Dixieland placed him in Carnegie Hall\, Germany\, Denmark\, Peru\, Japan\, and much of America. He has also performed solo in most of the states in the U.S. and in many foreign countries around the world. \nMcDermott is known for a style that mixes percussive drive of Professor Longhair\, the rhythmic sophistication of James Booker\, and the syncopated bounce of classic ragtime—then filters it all through his own imaginative voice.  Since 2001 he has made 16 trips to Brazil\, absorbing and recording choro\, a form often called “Brazilian ragtime\,” which he now weaves into his New Orleans repertoire\, creating a rich\, cross‑Atlantic sound. \nMcDermott has released 16 albums showcasing his work in solo piano\, small‑ensemble jazz such as the Dukes of Dixieland\, and inventive brass‑band projects.  His music has been praised by major publications such as The New York Times (five reviews)\, Rolling Stone\, Wall Street Journal\, and the Los Angeles Times. His tunes were licensed for the soundtrack of HBO’s Treme\, where he also appeared on camera as himself. \nWhen you hear Tom McDermott at the keyboard\, you’re not just getting a concert—you’re stepping into a living archive of New Orleans piano history\, seasoned with\, warmth\, and a touch of Brazilian flair. \nGet Tickets Here!
URL:https://www.pollyklaastheater.org/event/merging-music-humanity/
LOCATION:Polly Klaas Community Theater\, 417 Western Avenue\, Petaluma\, CA\, 94952\, United States
